# The declared conditions of the measurement host, as data.
#
# `preflight.sh` reads this file and asserts every value below against the live
# machine. `cpu-tuning.sh` reads the same file and applies them. One source, two
# consumers, so a condition cannot be applied and asserted differently — which
# is the failure mode of writing the conditions into prose and the tuning into a
# script, and `benchmarks/README.md#conditions` already says why it prefers a
# value read back off the live process to one transcribed.
#
# Changing a value here changes what the host IS, and every figure taken after
# the change was taken under different conditions. Say so in
# `benchmarks/results/` when you change one.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Identity — which machine this file describes
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
HOST_NAME=britton-ai
HOST_DESCRIPTION="Lenovo Legion 5 15ACH6, AMD Ryzen 7 5800H, 8C/16T, 15 GiB, Ubuntu 24.04.4"
EXPECTED_CPUS=16
EXPECTED_SCALING_DRIVER=amd-pstate-epp

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CPU regime
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `powersave` on amd-pstate-epp lets a core sit at 400 MHz between samples, so
# this looked like the single largest noise source and the plan that provisioned
# this host led with it.
#
# It was measured, and it is not. `benchmarks/results/flakiness/FLAKINESS.md`
# runs five criterion repetitions under each governor on this host: worst
# between-run spread 1.109x on powersave against 1.145x on performance, medians
# within 1%, indistinguishable. Criterion warms up before it measures and the
# workload keeps the core loaded, so amd-pstate ramps to its ceiling either way.
# What actually fixed the flakiness was removing the 21 unrelated containers --
# an Ollama inference server among them -- not this line.
#
# Kept anyway, on stated terms: it is free, `--restore` puts it back, and it may
# still matter for an instrument that takes discrete samples with idle gaps
# rather than a warmed-up batch. Do not cite it as the thing that worked.
GOVERNOR=performance

# A second, independent lever on this driver: the governor selects the
# algorithm, EPP biases it. Both must be set or the machine is half-tuned in a
# way that reads as tuned.
EPP=performance

# Empty means UNCAPPED, and empty is deliberate as the starting regime.
#
# Capping to the 5800H's 3.2 GHz base clock trades peak throughput for a clock
# that does not vary with die temperature, which is the right trade for a
# repository that quotes ratios between arms of one run. But capping BEFORE the
# flakiness is measured makes the measurement unable to say whether the cap was
# needed — and "we changed five things and it got better" is the shape of
# finding that this repository's own review process rejects. Set it after
# regime B is measured, if regime B says boost residency is the cause.
MAX_FREQ_KHZ=

# `on` or `off`. SMT off removes sibling contention and leaves 8 real cores,
# which is ample at `--test-threads=1`. Same argument as the cap: it is a second
# variable, and it moves in its own regime rather than alongside the governor.
SMT=on

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The clock the harness reads
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `hpet`, and the reason is the most expensive thing measured while provisioning
# this host. Read the whole of it before changing this line back.
#
# The kernel refuses this machine TSC at boot:
#
#     TSC synchronization [CPU#0 -> CPU#2]:
#     Measured 11225813293 cycles TSC warp between CPUs, turning off TSC clock.
#
# and falls back to hpet, where `clock_gettime(CLOCK_MONOTONIC)` leaves the vDSO
# fast path and costs a measured 1,390 ns against 19 ns on tsc -- a 73x penalty
# on the timer itself. That looked like a kernel false positive: 11.2e9 cycles is
# 3.5 seconds of apparent skew between two cores of one die, and the CPU
# advertises both constant_tsc and nonstop_tsc. So `tsc=reliable` went on the
# kernel command line and the machine was rebooted.
#
# It was wrong, and `ops/host/probes/tsc-migrate.c` is what said so. One thread,
# migrating between CPUs, comparing consecutive reads -- no concurrency to get
# wrong, and an SMT-sibling control that condemned two earlier multi-threaded
# attempts before this one was believed. On tsc it finds 62,493 backwards reads
# per million migrations, worst 2.0 ms. On hpet, same binary and same host, it
# finds ZERO in 500,000. That A/B is what makes the number evidence rather than
# an artefact: a broken instrument would have failed both.
#
# The proportions say what the shape is. Cycling all 16 CPUs gives 6.25%
# violations, which is 1/16 -- the wrap hop. Four cores gives 25%, which is 1/4.
# ONE physical core gives 50%: CPU0 and CPU1 are SMT siblings and they disagree
# by 6.46 ms. So each logical CPU carries a fixed TSC offset, and confining a
# measurement to one core does not escape it.
#
# And the mechanism is closed: `tsc_adjust` is NOT in /proc/cpuinfo on this part.
# That is the MSR Linux uses to correct per-CPU TSC offsets. Without it no kernel
# parameter can fix this -- only firmware. `tsc=reliable` has been removed from
# GRUB_CMDLINE_LINUX_DEFAULT so the kernel own check is the standing protection,
# and this line is the second one.
#
# What it costs, stated rather than hidden: at a 1,390 ns timer and paired.rs
# TIMER_HEADROOM of 10, arms below roughly 14 us are timer-dominated. Of the
# seven arms in results/raw/overhead.log exactly one is -- `memory` at a 5.8 us
# median. The other six sit at 143 us to 5.5 ms, where 1.4 us is under 1%. The
# criterion targets batch iterations and amortise it. The allocation counts,
# which benchmarks/README.md already says are the columns to quote, do not read a
# clock at all.
#
# Note what this variable holds: a clocksource NAME. preflight.sh compares the
# name and never a measured timer cost -- paired.rs owns that number, and
# asserting on it here would be the thing CF-34 forbids.
CLOCKSOURCE=hpet

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Memory and filesystem
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `always` changes allocator behaviour, and the allocation counts are the column
# `benchmarks/README.md` tells readers to quote in preference to the wall clock.
THP=madvise

# The SQLite arms create their databases under `std::env::temp_dir()`. On a host
# where that is a tmpfs they measure RAM and report it in a table headed by a
# filesystem — a different measurement wearing the same name. This is the Linux
# analogue of the Windows conditions row that pins %TEMP% to the internal NVMe.
TMPDIR_FSTYPE=ext4
MIN_FREE_GIB=20

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Quiet
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
MAX_LOADAVG_1=0.30
MAX_PKG_TEMP_C=60

# Containers permitted to be running during a measurement. The tunnel is idle
# and was kept deliberately; anything else is a neighbour.
ALLOWED_CONTAINERS=workspace-cloudflared

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Toolchain — the versions the gate needs present rather than skipped
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Must equal `rust-toolchain.toml`'s channel and the workspace `rust-version`.
RUSTC_VERSION=1.97.1
